{"causes": ["The destruction of Harran in 610 BCE by Median and Babylonian forces ignited Adad-guppi's lifelong devotion to the restoration of the Sîn cult.", "The decline of the Assyrian Empire created a power vacuum that allowed new regional powers like the Neo-Babylonian Empire to emerge, providing a context for her son's rise.", "The prevailing polytheistic religious landscape of Mesopotamia allowed for the elevation of specific deities like Sîn, which Adad-guppi actively championed."], "effects": ["Adad-guppi's influence contributed to Nabonidus's controversial religious reforms, which alienated the traditional Marduk priesthood in Babylon.", "Her advocacy for Sîn led to significant state resources being directed towards the rebuilding of the E-hulhul temple in Harran and other Sîn cult centers.", "Her political astuteness and connections were instrumental in the ascension of her son Nabonidus to the throne of the Neo-Babylonian Empire in 556 BCE."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "nabonidus", "sourceName": "Nabonidus", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "adad-guppi", "targetName": "Adad-guppi", "context": "Nabonidus's reign as king of the Neo-Babylonian Empire provided Adad-guppi with the platform to exert her religious and political influence."}, {"sourceSlug": "adad-guppi", "sourceName": "Adad-guppi", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "nabonidus", "targetName": "Nabonidus", "context": "Adad-guppi profoundly influenced Nabonidus's religious policies, particularly his devotion to the moon god Sîn, throughout his reign (556-539 BCE)."}, {"sourceSlug": "adad-guppi", "sourceName": "Adad-guppi", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "harran", "targetName": "Harran", "context": "Adad-guppi was a high priestess and central figure in the Sîn cult in the city of Harran, where her stelae were later discovered."}, {"sourceSlug": "adad-guppi", "sourceName": "Adad-guppi", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "cult-of-sin", "targetName": "Cult of Sîn", "context": "Adad-guppi was a lifelong and fervent devotee of the moon god Sîn, actively working for the restoration and prominence of his cult in Mesopotamia."}, {"sourceSlug": "neo-babylonian-empire", "sourceName": "Neo-Babylonian Empire", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "adad-guppi", "targetName": "Adad-guppi", "context": "Adad-guppi lived and exerted significant influence within the Neo-Babylonian Empire, especially during the reign of her son, King Nabonidus (556-539 BCE)."}], "places": [{"name": "Harran, Mesopotamia", "role": "Center of Sîn cult, Adad-guppi's home"}, {"name": "Babylon, Mesopotamia", "role": "Capital of son's empire"}, {"name": "Tayma, Arabia", "role": "Nabonidus's extended residence"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Adad-guppi", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "adad-guppi"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q346539", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adad-guppi"], "tags": ["Journalists & Chroniclers", "cause-and-effect", "cultural-diffusion", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Adad-guppi's profound influence on her son, King Nabonidus, significantly shaped the religious and political landscape of the Neo-Babylonian Empire in its final decades, contributing to internal dissent that weakened it before the Persian conquest. Her stelae provide a rare and invaluable female voice from ancient Mesopotamia, offering unique insights into the era's religious and dynastic politics.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-08-05T05:31:05Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30977129484", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Adad-guppi, also known as Addagoppe, was a devotee of the moon god Sîn in the northern Assyrian city of Harran, and the mother of King Nabonidus of the Neo-Babylonian Empire.", "newValue": "Adad-guppi (c. 650 – 545 BCE) was a high priestess and the influential mother of Nabonidus, the last king of the Neo-Babylonian Empire. Her life spanned the twilight of Assyrian power and the rise and fall of the Neo-Babylonian state, making her a unique figure bridging the cultural and religious traditions of the ancient Near East. A fervent devotee of the moon god Sîn in the northern Mesopotamian city of Harran, she profoundly influenced her son's controversial religious and political policies, positioning Sîn as a paramount deity.

Born around 650 BCE, Adad-guppi lived through the destruction of Harran by the Medes and Babylonians in 610 BCE, an event that deeply shaped her religious convictions. She dedicated her life to the restoration of the Sîn cult and the rebuilding of the E-hulhul temple in Harran. Her political acumen and connections were instrumental in her son, Nabonidus, ascending to the Neo-Babylonian throne in 556 BCE. Throughout his reign (556–539 BCE), Adad-guppi served as a powerful advisor, especially in religious matters, advocating for the prominence of Sîn over Marduk, the traditional chief god of Babylon. Her stelae, discovered in Harran, provide rare autobiographical details, including her claim of living for 104 years and serving four kings.

